AIM:
Configure and analyse Static Routing using Cisco Packet Tracer.

STEP 1: CREATE TOPOLOGY
Add:
- 2 Routers
- 2 Switches
- 4 PCs

Arrange:
PC0, PC1 -> Switch0 -> Router0
Router0 -> Router1
Router1 -> Switch1 -> PC2, PC3

STEP 3: CONFIGURE PC IP ADDRESSES

PC0:
IP Address: 192.168.1.2
Subnet Mask: 255.255.255.0
Default Gateway: 192.168.1.1


PC2:
IP Address: 192.168.2.2
Subnet Mask: 255.255.255.0
Default Gateway: 192.168.2.1


STEP 4: CONFIGURE ROUTER0

Open Router0 -> Config -> FastEthernet0/0

IP Address: 192.168.1.1
Subnet Mask: 255.255.255.0
Port Status: ON

STEP 5: CONFIGURE ROUTER1

Open Router1 -> Config -> FastEthernet0/0

IP Address: 192.168.2.1
Subnet Mask: 255.255.255.0
Port Status: ON

STEP 6: CONFIGURE SERIAL CONNECTION

Router0 -> Config -> Serial2/0

IP Address: 10.10.0.2
Subnet Mask: 255.0.0.0
Clock Rate: 64000
Port Status: ON

Router1 -> Config -> Serial2/0

IP Address: 10.10.0.3
Subnet Mask: 255.0.0.0
Clock Rate: Not Set
Port Status: ON

STEP 7: VERIFY INTERFACES

On both routers, open CLI and enter:

enable
show ip interface brief

Check that FastEthernet0/0 and Serial2/0
show UP/UP.

If an interface is administratively down:

configure terminal
interface fastEthernet 0/0
no shutdown
exit
end

STEP 8: CONFIGURE STATIC ROUTING ON ROUTER0

Open Router0 -> CLI:

enable
configure terminal
ip route 192.168.2.0 255.255.255.0 10.10.0.3
end

STEP 9: CONFIGURE STATIC ROUTING ON ROUTER1

Open Router1 -> CLI:

enable
configure terminal
ip route 192.168.1.0 255.255.255.0 10.10.0.2
end

STEP 10: VERIFY ROUTING TABLE

On both routers, enter:

show ip route

Look for routes marked with S.

Router0:
S 192.168.2.0/24 via 10.10.0.3

Router1:
S 192.168.1.0/24 via 10.10.0.2

S = Static Route

STEP 11: TEST CONNECTIVITY

Open PC0 -> Desktop -> Command Prompt.

Enter:

ping 192.168.2.2